#include <markdownsharp/markdown.h>
#include <string>

std::string convertMarkdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    using namespace MarkdownSharp;

    // Create a new Markdown converter instance
    Converter converter;

    // Convert the Markdown text to HTML
    string/html htmlText = converter.Convert(markdownText);

    return htmlText;
}
```

In this example, we include the headers for `MarkdownSharp`, create a `Converter` object, pass the input Markdown text to its `Convert()` method, and retrieve the resulting HTML text as a `string/html` object.

Note that you'll need to install the MarkdownSharp library in your project. You can do this using a package manager like vcpkg or Conan, or by downloading the prebuilt binaries from the MarkdownSharp website.

Also, keep in mind that this is just one possible implementation, and there may be other Markdown converters available for C++. If you prefer another library or approach, feel free to modify the code accordingly!

Call this function by passing a `std::string` containing Markdown text, like so:
```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# Hello World!";
    std::string htmlText = convertMarkdownToHtml(markdownText);
    // Use the converted HTML text...
    return 0;
}
```

